{% set local_problem %}
<p>Lake Guntersville debris is different from what you'd clean out of gutters in Huntsville. The humidity changes everything.</p>

<p>Normal debris dries out between rains. Eventually it's light enough to blow away or comes out easily when cleaned. Lake debris never fully dries. It absorbs moisture from fog, from humidity, from the air itself. It compacts. It decomposes. Within a few months, what started as leaves has turned into a dense organic layer — we call it "gutter concrete" — that weighs down the gutters, holds water against the metal, and doesn't come out without real effort.</p>

<p>We cleaned a house in <a href="/recent-projects/">Brown's Creek</a> where the homeowner had hired someone else first. That crew had spent 15 minutes with a leaf blower and left. The gutters still had 3 inches of compacted debris in them — the blower just moved the loose stuff on top. We spent 2 hours hand-removing material that had basically turned to soil, then flushed downspouts that were 80% blocked with debris that had washed down and impacted.</p>

<p>That's the difference between cleaning for the receipt and cleaning to actually solve the problem.</p>

<p>For vacation homes on <a href="/recent-projects/">Buck Island</a>, along <a href="/recent-projects/">North Lake Drive</a>, or anywhere you're not monitoring regularly, deferred cleaning creates expensive problems. Small clogs become complete blockages. Overflow starts damaging fascia. Debris weight stresses hangers. By the time you notice, you've got repairs on top of the cleaning.</p>
{% endset %}

{% set service_explanation %}
<p>Here's what we actually do, step by step:</p>

<p><strong>Debris removal by hand.</strong> We scoop debris out of gutters into buckets. Not blow it onto your roof to wash back in. Not scatter it across your lawn. Remove it and dispose of it. On lake properties with compacted debris, this takes 45-75 minutes.</p>

<p><strong>Downspout flush.</strong> Every downspout gets water run through it until it flows clear at the bottom. If it's clogged, we clear it — snake, pressure, whatever it takes. This is the step most cheap cleanings skip, and it's where 40% of drainage problems actually live. Another 15-25 minutes.</p>

<p><strong>System inspection.</strong> While we're up there, we check: gutter pitch (should be 1/4" drop per 10 feet toward downspouts), hanger condition (spacing should be 24" max, 18" in high-humidity areas), seam integrity, and fascia condition behind the gutters. Lake homes get extra attention here — we're looking for humidity damage that mainland homes don't experience. Another 15-20 minutes.</p>

<p><strong>Documentation.</strong> For vacation homes, we photograph before, during, and after. Every problem area gets documented. You get the photos same-day by email or text. You know exactly what's happening with your property without being there.</p>

<p><strong>Ground cleanup.</strong> Whatever debris we dropped getting it out of the gutters, we clean up. 10-15 minutes.</p>

<p>Total time: 90 minutes to 2+ hours for most lake homes. Anyone quoting 30 minutes isn't doing this. They're skimming the surface and leaving the real problems behind.</p>
{% endset %}

